.TH wc "" "Command"
.SH NAME
wc \- count words, lines, and characters in files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBwc [\-clw] [\fIfile ...\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
wc -- count words, lines, and characters in files
Usage:	wc [ -clw ] [ file ... ]
Options:
	-c	Print count of characters
	-l	Print count of lines
	-w	Print count of words
If no 'file' is given, it uses the standard input;
if more than one 'file' is given, it also prints a total.
.HE
For each named
.IR file ,
.B wc
counts and prints the number of lines, words, and characters it
contains.
A word is a maximal run of characters delimited by a blank, a tab, or
a newline; a line is counted at each newline character read.
.PP
If no
.I file
is named,
.B wc
reads the standard input and prints a single count with no file name
attached.
If more than one
.I file
is named,
.B wc
prints a count for each, in argument order, followed by a line labeled
.B Total
that sums the counts over all of them.
A named file that cannot be opened is reported and skipped, and does
not contribute to the total.
.IP \fB\-l\fR
Print only the line count.
.IP \fB\-w\fR
Print only the word count.
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Print only the character count.
.PP
The
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-w ,
and
.B \-c
options may be combined in one argument, e.g.
.B \-lw
prints both the line and word counts.
If none of them is given,
.B wc
prints all three counts, in the order lines, words, characters.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B wc
prints
.B "wc: can't open file"
for each named file it cannot open, and goes on to the next file.
.SH NOTES
Each count is printed right-justified in an eight-character field; if a
file name follows, it is preceded by four spaces.
